<Base title="About | Federica Iengo" description="Meet Federica Iengo: curiosity, data engineering, AI Search and a practical approach to understanding complex information.">
<section class="about-hero">
<p class="eyebrow">About / Federica Iengo</p>
<h1>I like finding answers. I might like finding <span class="title-accent-bronze">new questions</span> even more.</h1>
<div class="personal-intro"><p>I enjoy solving problems, but I'm just as interested in discovering new ones.</p><p>I tend to find questions in almost everything I explore. Sometimes an answer leads to a solution; other times, it reveals a problem I hadn't considered before.</p><p>I believe understanding is often a matter of perspective. Change the way you look at something, and patterns that once seemed invisible begin to emerge.</p><p>There's something beautiful about finding structure in complexity. And sometimes, the most interesting discoveries come from asking a different question.</p></div>
</section>
<section class="split" aria-labelledby="work">
<div><p class="eyebrow">What I work on</p><h2 id="work">Different fields. The same curiosity.</h2></div>
<div class="prose"><h3>Data engineering and analytics</h3><p>I use SQL, PostgreSQL, Snowflake, dbt, Python and pandas to build and test data pipelines, model data and reconcile figures. I care about making complex data reliable and understandable, not just producing the final chart.</p>
<h3>AI Search, SEO, AEO and GEO</h3><p>I research how information is structured, found, retrieved, cited and presented in AI-generated answers. I build controlled comparisons, investigate possible improvements and keep proposed changes distinct from measured outcomes.</p>
<p>These interests overlap. Both require clear definitions, reliable sources, repeatable checks and a willingness to change your mind when the evidence changes.</p></div>
</section>
<section class="approach" aria-labelledby="approach"><p class="eyebrow">How I approach a problem</p><h2 id="approach">Make the reasoning inspectable.</h2><div class="tiles"><article><span>01</span><h3>Understand the question</h3><p>Clarify what a useful answer would actually change, rather than starting with a tool or metric.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Follow the evidence</h3><p>Check source quality, definitions and edge cases. Separate what is observed from what is inferred.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Build something repeatable</h3><p>Document the method so another person can inspect, challenge or extend the work.</p></article></div></section>
<section class="beyond" aria-labelledby="beyond"><div><p class="eyebrow">Beyond the immediate brief</p><h2 id="beyond">Curiosity doesn't fit neatly into a job title.</h2></div><p>My interests extend beyond data and search to artificial intelligence, software systems, quantum computing and scientific research. I enjoy the ideas behind emerging technologies, especially when they challenge an assumption. I don't confuse curiosity with expertise: curiosity is what keeps me learning.</p><p>I appreciate Occam's razor. Although sometimes, before simplifying a problem, I can't resist finding three more questions inside it.</p></section>
<section class="cta"><p class="eyebrow">Explore the work</p><h2>See the methods in practice.</h2><p>Projects and research notes include the questions, limitations and evidence behind the work, not just polished conclusions.</p><div class="links"><a href="/projects/">Explore projects →</a><a href="/research/">Read research notes →</a><a href="https://github.com/federicaiengo" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub profile ↗</a></div></section>
</Base>
